\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The Hessian route remains an open test}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The Hessian proposal is the route recorded in
[the Hessian-group outlook](fgap-001X). That remark begins
with
##{Q_8\rtimes C_3\cong 2T}
and then names the iterated semidirect product
#{(G_{27}\rtimes Q_8)\rtimes C_3}. The actions in those
products are part of the data. The name of the product does
not choose them. The same remark states that a central scalar
subgroup #{C_3\leq G_{27}} has quotient the Hessian group of
order 216, and that the larger group is a triple cover of that
quotient. This chapter does not reconstruct #{G_{27}}, the
semidirect actions, or that quotient.}

\p{The known algebraic datum is the binary tetrahedral group
already used in [the finite spinor identities](fdip-00A0),
together with the presentation named above. The missing map
is a typed homomorphism from the iterated product, or from
its order-216 quotient, into #{U(1)\times SU(2)\times SU(3)}
or into a group already constructed in these notes.
The comparison with #{U(1)\times SU(2)\times SU(3)} stays an
attributed proposal in \ref{fgap-001X}. It is not an
isomorphism, and the comparison motivation recorded beside
that outlook is not a construction of the finite group.}

\p{The finite witness available now is #{2T} alone. It does
not determine #{G_{27}} or the order-216 quotient. A witness
that would meet the [compatibility datum](fdip-009Z) would
be a concrete model of one of those groups, a representation
on a named fiber, a selector, and one reference object.
None of those slots is filled. Every criterion is open.
That absence is not a no-go theorem and not a physical
identification.}
